      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The rigorous mathematical underpinning of the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle is most elegantly derived by considering the wave nature of quantum particles, specifically through the lens of wave packets, which are localized superpositions of momentum eigenstates. A wave packet, which represents a particle with a finite, measurable position, must be constructed by summing (integrating) over a range of different momentum components, each corresponding to a plane wave with a specific wave number $k$. The mathematical relationship between the position space representation $\\psi(x)$ and the momentum space representation $\\phi(p)$ is precisely governed by the Fourier transform: $\\phi(p) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi\\hbar}} \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\psi(x) e^{-ipx/\\hbar} dx$. The inherent uncertainty arises because any function $\\psi(x)$ that is localized in position space (i.e., has a narrow $\\Delta x$) must necessarily be composed of a broad spectrum of momenta, meaning its Fourier transform $\\phi(p)$ must be spread out in momentum space ($\\Delta p$ is large). Conversely, a perfectly defined momentum state (a pure plane wave, $\\Delta p = 0$) is infinitely spread out in position space ($\\Delta x",
